samir: opened a PR with a compact playbook for JSON fallbacks + SDK shims: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/642 — want eyes on the examples and migration steps

maria: on it. quick q — do we keep the old compact-envelope examples or fully replace them with the new recommended shape? :thinking:

samir: keep both for now. docs should show legacy->shim->recommended flow so customers can migrate gradually.

jonah: small nit on the schema in the PR: lots of anyOf at top-level. that breaks our TS codegen and makes parsing fragile. can we provide a canonical "stable" shape for SDKs to rely on?

eli: yep. adding TS + Python friendly types in the PR. sample: 
```ts
// recommended response shape
type ActionResponse = {
  action: string;
  args?: Record<string, unknown>;
  confidence?: number;
};
```

```py
@dataclass
class ActionResponse:
    action: str
    args: Dict[str, Any] = field(default_factory=dict)
    confidence: Optional[float] = None
```

jonah: that helps. also add a short migration recipe for converting older union/array envelopes into the stable object.

ci-bot: link-checker failed: docs/reference/tool-calling.md -> 404 (line 123)

maria: noted, will update the link to /docs/tooling/tool-calling.md and push a fix to the PR in a minute.

samir: request: add a tiny guardrail checklist at top of the doc: prefer top-level object, one action per model output, and a clear tool_call object when invoking a tool. keeps parsing deterministic.

maria: draft guardrail copy: 
- "Response MUST be a top-level JSON object."
- "If invoking a tool, include a single `tool_call` object with `name` and `arguments`."
- "Avoid arrays-of-variants at the top level (use `items` inside a named property if you need lists)."

eli: adding a quick SDK helper to normalize responses before consumers hit them: 
```js
// normalize response
function normalize(resp) {
  if (Array.isArray(resp)) return resp[0];
  if (resp?.tool_call?.arguments && typeof resp.tool_call.arguments === 'string') {
    try { resp.tool_call.arguments = JSON.parse(resp.tool_call.arguments) } catch {}
  }
  return resp;
}
```

maria: good call. I'm also adding a "test prompt" in the doc so reviewers can reproduce common failures. example prompt text: 
"Call the weather API for city \"Paris\" and return a JSON with `tool_call` containing `name: \"weather.get\"` and `arguments` as an object."

samir: add an eval checklist: streaming partial JSON, truncated tokens, and malformed strings. tiny acceptance criteria for each.

jonah: and include a pragmatic parser fallback example in the migration guide: 
```py
# fallback parse
try:
  data = json.loads(output)
except JSONDecodeError:
  # try to extract the first JSON object in the text
  m = re.search(r'\{.*\}', output, re.S)
  data = json.loads(m.group(0)) if m else {}
```

eli: one more: update the SDK snippets index link to /docs/sdk/snippets/tool_helpers.md or CI will fail.

maria: just pushed link fixes + TS/Python examples + guardrail bullets. PTAL.

samir: I'll review tonight. if no major blockers we can merge Friday and cut a docs-site rebuild. :rocket:

jonah: small follow-up request: add a note about size limits for tool arguments — we saw large payloads blow past KV cache and increase latency. recommendation: keep args < 8KB where possible.

maria: added that as a migration note with a sample trimming helper.

samir: tagging ops so Dedicated rollout teams see the change. @ops-bot FYI.

